[h1]Lizarb Interface[/h1]

A complete reskin of RimWorld 1.6's interface. Window frames, buttons, tabs, tooltips, checkboxes, gizmos, scrollbars, fonts and the Architect menu.

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/themes.png[/img]

[h2]16 themes[/h2]

A theme is not just a palette. Each one changes the corner radius, the weight of the metal fillet, the corner ornament and the background pattern, so the shapes read as differently as the colours do.

[list]
[*][b]Iron[/b] is square and austere.
[*][b]Royal[/b] is wide and heavy, indigo and gold.
[*][b]Aero[/b] is a translucent bubble with no outline at all.
[*][b]Gothic[/b] carries a trefoil in every corner.
[*][b]Wood[/b] has a planed bevel instead of forged metal, because a board is sawn, not turned.
[/list]

Seven tileable background patterns, baked per theme so they carry that theme's own inks rather than a flat tint. Pick one for free: choosing a theme applies its pattern, and you can swap it afterwards.

[h2]The Architect menu[/h2]

[img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/dequevedo/lizarb-interface/main/docs/architect.png[/img]

Every category button is coloured by what the category is for. The colours come from twelve families rather than the whole hue circle, and categories doing related jobs share one on purpose: that similarity is what keeps thirty buttons from reading as noise.

Categories added by other mods need no patch. They are matched on their own name, so Storage lands with storage and Genetics with the medical group.

The menu also sizes itself to its longest label, in whatever font and size you picked, so nothing clips.

[h2]Fonts[/h2]

Twenty-eight faces ship inside the mod. Nothing to install and nothing to download: every player gets the same list. Any font already on your machine can be picked as well, and the picker previews each name in its own face.

Three text sizes are adjustable, and every label can carry a real outline at a chosen opacity. The outline is eight offsets at one screen pixel, not a blurred shadow, so it stays a ring instead of a smudge.

[h2]Compatibility[/h2]

This mod replaces the drawing of tabs, window backgrounds and menu sections rather than adding to them. Another mod that redraws those same surfaces will lose whichever of the two loads first, so load this one last if you want its look to win.

Buttons, window frames, tabs, widgets and scrollbars can each be handed back to the game on their own. A clash with one other mod costs one checkbox instead of the whole skin.

Prefer the vanilla look for a while? The theme grid has a [b]RimWorld default[/b] entry that returns every surface, and the font, to the game.

[h2]Source and licence[/h2]

